\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Anti-Gay Bill Spurs Walkout, Rattling \uc2 Disney\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brooks Barnes \par \uc2 1,280 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hoping to avoid controversy, the company initially shied away from taking a public stance on the anti-L.G.B.T.Q. bill. Now, an internal outcry has stretched into its third week.\par \par \uc2 Several weeks ago, before the \uc2 Walt Disney Company\uc2  became entangled in a battle over anti-L.G.B.T.Q. legislation in Florida -- one that continued on Tuesday with walkouts by \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees -- longtime theme park executives worried there could be trouble ahead.\par \par \uc2 More than 150 companies, including \uc2 Marriott\uc2  and \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , had signed a \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  letter opposing the legislation, which restricts classroom instruction on sexual orientation and gender identity and has been labeled by opponents as the ''Don't Say Gay'' bill. \uc2 Disney\uc2 , with roughly 80,000 theme park workers in Florida and a long history of supporting the L.G.B.T.Q. community, was not among them. Leaders at the Disney Parks, Experiences and Products division urged action: \uc2 Disney\uc2 's name should be on the list.\par \par \uc2 They were rebuffed, according to three people briefed on the matter, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to comply with company strictures about speaking to reporters. \uc2 Disney\uc2 's newly hired corporate affairs chief, \uc2 Geoff Morrell\uc2 , and \uc2 Disney\uc2  legislative affairs executives -- guided by the general desire of \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2 , the chief executive, to avoid publicly weighing in on state political battles -- decided that continuing to work behind the scenes had a better chance of a payoff. For weeks, \uc2 Disney\uc2  lobbyists in Florida had been pressing to soften the legislation.\par \par \uc2 At least in this instance, staying quiet backfired, resulting in a cascade of events that has amounted to one of the biggest squalls for \uc2 Disney\uc2  in decades. It has also become a high-profile example of a stark shift in corporate culture: A socially conscious generation of workers are demanding that their employers speak out on contentious social and political issues.\par \par \uc2 Faced with an employee uprising about the company's decision to stay quiet, Mr. Chapek shifted course and publicly disavowed the Florida legislation on March 9, the day of \uc2 Disney\uc2 's annual shareholder meeting. On March 10, Gov. Ron DeSantis punched back, mocking the company as ''Woke Disney.'' On March 11, with some employees still angry and questions mounting about his leadership, Mr. Chapek bluntly apologized to \uc2 Disney\uc2 's 200,000 workers in an email.\par \par \uc2 Last week, still-unsatisfied \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees began organizing protests with a website, WhereIsChapek.com. On Friday, \uc2 Walt Disney Studios\uc2  let it be known that a same-sex kiss that had been cut from \uc2 Pixar\uc2 's upcoming ''Lightyear'' had been restored. Over the weekend, announcers at \uc2 Disney\uc2 -owned \uc2 ESPN\uc2  protested the legislation with on-air silence during the \uc2 N.C.A.A\uc2 . women's basketball tournament.\par \par \uc2 And now \uc2 Disney\uc2  -- a company that cultivates perfection as part of its brand -- finds itself in the third week of a mess.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the WhereIsChapek employee protests culminated with various actions. None were particularly boisterous, although they succeeded in commanding the news media's attention. On Tuesday morning, a CNBC crew reported live from the front gates of \uc2 Disney\uc2 's corporate headquarters in Burbank, Calif.\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 Disney\uc2  artists posted support on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , where the hashtag #\uc2 DisneySayGay\uc2  was prominent midmorning. Sixty to 70 \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees briefly walked in a loop around \uc2 Walt Disney Studios\uc2  in Burbank. Down the street at the Bette Davis Picnic Area, a smattering of \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees gathered in protest, although they seemed outnumbered, at least at one point, by members of the news media.\par \par \uc2 In a statement about the walkouts on Tuesday, the company said, ''We respect our colleagues' right to express their views, and we pledge our ongoing support of the L.G.B.T.Q.+ community in the fight for equal rights.''\par \par \uc2 It was impossible to gauge the ultimate level of participation; most \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees are still working at home. Some participation was virtual, one organizer said, with employees leaving an ''away'' message on Slack or other internal messaging systems to express solidarity.\par \par \uc2 The creators of the WhereIsChapek site said they were members of \uc2 Disney\uc2 's L.G.B.T.Q. ''community and their allies.'' The site listed demands, including indefinitely ceasing -- not pausing and re-evaluating, as Mr. Chapek has promised -- political donations to Florida lawmakers who were involved in the passage of the bill. The New York Times verified that the anonymous organizers were \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees.\par \par \uc2 To get ahead of the walkout, \uc2 Disney\uc2  held an all-company virtual town hall on Monday that was dedicated to L.G.B.T.Q. issues. (A town hall had long been planned for Tuesday, albeit on a different diversity, equity and inclusion topic.) The roughly 100-minute session featured a panel discussion with eight \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees who spoke about their own L.G.B.T.Q. experience and why the company's initial silence on the bill was hurtful. Nadine Smith, executive director of Equality Florida, a civil rights advocacy group, participated in a separate discussion.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chapek spoke briefly on camera, according to several people who attended the meeting.\par \par \uc2 ''I understand where we have made mistakes -- and the pain those mistakes caused,'' he said. ''I know that our silence wasn't just about the bill in Florida, but about every time an individual or institution that should have stood up for this community didn't. I and the leadership team are determined to use this moment as a catalyst for more meaningful and lasting change.''\par \par \uc2 As part of the town hall, \uc2 Disney\uc2  announced that Mr. Chapek had postponed a management retreat planned for next week in Orlando, Fla. He and senior executives would instead use that time to go on a listening tour at \uc2 Disney\uc2  workplaces, both domestically and overseas.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  also unveiled a task force to develop an action plan for \uc2 Disney\uc2  to be a more positive force for the L.G.B.T.Q. community, including through its content for families. In addition, \uc2 Disney\uc2  said it had signed on to the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2 's condemnation of anti-transgender government actions in Texas.\par \par \uc2 To understand the furor inside \uc2 Disney\uc2  about its handling of the legislation in Florida, it helps to know \uc2 Disney\uc2 's history.\par \par \uc2 In the 1990s, \uc2 Disney\uc2  was one of the first major corporations to offer health coverage to the live-in partners of gay and lesbian employees. That decision, paired with tolerance of an unofficial ''gay day'' celebration at Walt Disney World in Florida, prompted a noisy boycott from Southern Baptists. \uc2 Disney\uc2  stood firm. (Church members officially ended their boycott in 2005.)\par \par \uc2 In the current situation, \uc2 Disney\uc2  not only tried to stay quiet, it had given money to Florida politicians supporting the legislation, raising alarm, particularly among longtime L.G.B.T.Q. employees: Was \uc2 Disney\uc2  fading as an ally?\par \par \uc2 There has also been simmering resentment inside \uc2 Disney\uc2  over L.G.B.T.Q. representation in \uc2 Disney\uc2 -branded content. It did not start with Mr. Chapek's tenure. \uc2 Disney\uc2  has long tried to keep such characters and relationships to a minimum. It had been decided that \uc2 Pixar\uc2 's ''Lightyear,'' for instance, could have a lesbian couple at its center. But a G-rated kiss was perhaps a step too far.\par \par \uc2 These factors and others -- the prolonged isolation of \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees during the pandemic, perhaps -- added to an already combustible situation.\par \par \uc2 Remy Tumin contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Remy Tumin contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Employees at Disney headquarters in Burbank, Calif., left their posts on Tuesday to protest the company's failure to publicly disavow anti-L.G.B.T.Q. legislation later passed by Florida lawmakers. (PHOTOGRAPH BY J. EMILIO FLORES FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B4) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220323ei3n00042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
