\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 BuzzFeed Employees Charge That I.P.O. Cheated Them of Millions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson \par \uc2 903 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The group is asking for compensatory damages estimated at more than $8.7 million.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 80 former and current employees of \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  accused the company in complaints on Tuesday of bungling its stock market debut and denying the workers the chance to sell their shares at a higher price.\par \par \uc2 In two claims, made to the \uc2 American Arbitration Association\uc2 , which resolves disputes out of court, the employees said the company had failed to properly instruct them on how to trade their shares immediately after the initial public offering in December.\par \par \uc2 The groups are asking for compensatory damages estimated at more than $8.7 million, according to the claims, which were viewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The Kafkaesque tribulations through which the claimants were dragged have wreaked havoc on their financial lives,'' one complaint said.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  spokesman said in a statement on Tuesday that ''\uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  prioritized communication with former and current employees last year to provide them with the information they needed to manage their equity.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's regrettable that the stock price declined, but there is no merit to the claims and we intend to rebut them vigorously,'' the spokesman added.\par \par \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2 , a news and entertainment publisher, began trading on Dec. 6. The company's stock price fell sharply in the days after it went public, and employees say they were not able to sell their shares until the price had dropped nearly 60 percent, to less than $5.\par \par \uc2 Some workers are still unable to sell their shares, according to the complaints.\par \par \uc2 The arbitration actions represent 77 employees, who collectively had more than 900,000 shares of \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  stock when it went public. The two claims were filed separately before the \uc2 American Arbitration Association\uc2  because a clause in the employees' contracts requires certain disputes to go to arbitration instead of court. That clause is common in employment contracts as an effort to prevent class-action lawsuits. Arbitration claims are decided by an impartial third party, though many are settled before that decision.\par \par \uc2 In addition to naming \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  and some of its top executives as defendants, including its founder, \uc2 Jonah Peretti\uc2 , the complaints name \uc2 Adam Rothstein\uc2 , the executive chairman of a shell company that merged with \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2 , and Continental Stock Transfer, a transfer agent hired to help with its initial public offering.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rothstein and \uc2 Continental\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  was co-founded by Mr. Peretti in 2006. According to the claims, the employees, which includes reporters, web developers, editors and salespeople, had mostly joined \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  in its early days when it was a scrappy start-up. They accepted low salaries because they were also given stock options, the employees said, and Mr. Peretti frequently promoted the eventual plan to take the company public.\par \par \uc2 In June last year, \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  announced its plans to merge with a special purpose acquisition company, or SPAC, called 890 Fifth Avenue Partners in order to go public. The deal valued \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  at $1.5 billion. The company is worth about one-third of that now.\par \par \uc2 By the time of the merger in December, about 94 percent of the over $250 million raised by the SPAC was withdrawn by investors, leaving the company with only $16 million. The complaints argued that because of this, \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  executives had a fiduciary duty to re-evaluate the plans to go public. But the I.P.O. went ahead, and \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  began trading on Nasdaq on Dec. 6 under the ticker symbol BZFD.\par \par \uc2 The employees, the claims said, were looking forward to finally cashing in their shares but quickly realized that they were unable to do so because they had not been told extra steps were needed to convert their Class B shares before they could sell them.\par \par \uc2 The blunder is not associated with what is commonly known as a ''lockup'' agreement that prevents top executives from selling shares for a specified period, typically about six months. In this case, employees could sell as soon as they filed necessary paperwork ahead of the public debut, but they were not given enough time to complete the application until after the company hit the stock market, the employees say.\par \par \uc2 Communications from \uc2 Continental\uc2  and \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  offered contradictory and vague advice about the stock transfers, according to the complaints, and employees were told the conversion of shares would take three to five business days. At the same time, \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2 's stock price, which had spiked in early trading, was rapidly dropping.\par \par \uc2 ''As a result, claimants -- some of whom still are unable to trade their shares as of the date of this filing -- lost the opportunity to sell their hard-earned shares for good value and have been left with stock trading at a mere fraction of its I.P.O. price,'' one of the complaints said.\par \par \uc2 One employee texted with Mr. Peretti on Dec. 6 to express his frustration, and, according to the complaint, Mr. Peretti complained that he had not been able to cash out his shares at as high a price as he had hoped, either. On Dec. 7, \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  emailed employees and said they ''sympathize with your frustration with this process.''\par \par \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  will report earnings next Tuesday for the first time since going public.\par \par \uc2 BuzzFeed's chief executive, Jonah Peretti, center, celebrating its listing. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BENNETT RAGLIN/GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220316ei3g0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
