\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Minneapolis Teachers Go on Strike, Closing Schools for 30,000 Students\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacey Fortin \par \uc2 1,085 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The union is asking for smaller class sizes and higher wages, while the school district says its budget is shrinking because of declining enrollment. After negotiations, St. Paul averted a strike.\par \par \uc2 Teachers in Minneapolis went on strike Tuesday morning, shuttering classrooms for about 30,000 public school students.\par \par \uc2 For weeks, the teachers' union and school district officials have been negotiating over salaries, hiring and resources for students' mental health. The talks in Minneapolis failed to reach a resolution by their Monday evening deadline, with the district saying it could not afford to meet teachers' demands.\par \par \uc2 Megan Peterson, a first-grade teacher, demonstrated in southern Minneapolis on Tuesday morning while her son, a first grader, stayed home with her husband.\par \par \uc2 ''It is not something any of us want to be doing right now,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''But this is important for me as a parent,'' she added. ''This is important for me as a teacher. And it's important for me, as a resident of Minneapolis, for me to make sure my kids are taken care of.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Monday evening, the district said it ''would remain at the mediation table nonstop in an effort to reduce the length and impact of this strike.''\par \par \uc2 In St. Paul, the schools were open on Tuesday. A teachers' strike was averted after the union there, the Saint Paul Federation of Educators, reached a tentative agreement with Saint Paul Public Schools on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 ''I believe we have arrived at fair and equitable agreements that respect our collective desire to do right by our students,'' the superintendent of Saint Paul Public Schools, Joe Gothard, said in a statement, ''while working within the district's budget and enrollment limitations.''\par \par \uc2 Students in the Twin Cities have already faced pandemic-related disruptions this year. In January, students in Minneapolis learned remotely for two weeks because of staff shortages related to the coronavirus. In St. Paul, some schools also returned to virtual learning for days at a time.\par \par \uc2 The strike on Tuesday left some Minneapolis parents scrambling to find child care.\par \par \uc2 A Monday evening robocall informed Brett Otteson, a father of three, that his children's classes would be canceled on Tuesday. He found day care at a nearby church, but he is unsure what to expect in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 ''The pandemic, as arbitrary as it seemed, was at least justifiable,'' he said. ''This just seems cruel and indefensible. It's an unforced error.''\par \par \uc2 Jessica Sutherland, whose three sons stayed with their grandmother on Tuesday, said the closure ''kind of felt inevitable, in a weird way.''\par \par \uc2 She continued: ''We haven't had any normalcy in so long, why would we have any normalcy now?''\par \par \uc2 Her children's teachers, she added, seemed to be doing the best they could to keep parents informed. ''At no point during any of this have I ever felt like I saw a teacher giving up,'' she said, ''during the pandemic and now with the strike.''\par \par \uc2 As schools across the United States returned from winter break during the Omicron surge, many teachers' unions raised concerns about understaffing because of illness, and shortages of masks and tests. In Chicago, home to the country's third-largest school district, a week of classes were canceled after teachers' union members argued that classrooms were unsafe. Schools reopened after a deal was announced on Jan. 10.\par \par \uc2 But pandemic-related issues have not been the sole source of disagreement between the Minneapolis teachers' union and the school district.\par \par \uc2 Members of the union have asked for more competitive salaries for teachers, a starting salary of $35,000 for most education support professionals, better conditions to recruit and retain educators of color, and enough staff to address students' mental health needs.\par \par \uc2 Greta Callahan, who leads the teachers' chapter of the Minneapolis Federation of Teachers, said significant change was needed in her district.\par \par \uc2 ''They continue to look at our proposals and say, 'These are add-ons that we can't afford.' And we're saying, 'No, you need to rewrite the whole system and do things differently.'''\par \par \uc2 Ed Graff, the superintendent of Minneapolis Public Schools, has said that the district shared many of the same goals. But the district said it had been hamstrung by falling enrollment numbers, which means cuts to school budgets.\par \par \uc2 The district, he said, ''continues to face a significant gap between the resources we have -- our revenue -- and the financial commitments we made -- our expenses,'' in part because of falling enrollment, rising costs and decades of underfunding.\par \par \uc2 Total enrollment in the city's public schools from kindergarten through 12th grade fell to just below 30,000 at the start of this school year, down from nearly 33,600 in the fall of 2019.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Graff added that coronavirus relief funds from the federal government were helping the district address budget shortfalls, but would not be enough to cover long-term expenses like salary increases.\par \par \uc2 The teachers' union has pointed to budget surpluses in Minnesota and said that within the district, money and power have been concentrated at the top while educators have struggled to do more with less.\par \par \uc2 St. Paul schools also face an issue of declining enrollment. About 34,000 students enrolled in the school district in 2021, down from 37,000 in the fall of 2019. The decline prompted a December decision to close and consolidate a handful of schools.\par \par \uc2 According to the union, the tentative agreement reached on Monday increases resources dedicated to students' mental health, solidifies language about limiting class sizes and increases compensation, especially for educational assistants.\par \par \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 , said that the unions in both cities had been especially concerned with securing fair pay for educational support staff. She also criticized Minneapolis's approach to negotiations.\par \par \uc2 ''The difference between the bargaining this weekend in St. Paul and Minneapolis were night and day,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 It has been decades since Minneapolis teachers last went on strike. Educators in St. Paul last went on strike in 2020 in a bid for more resources to improve mental health support for students and address racial disparities, among other issues.\par \par \uc2 Jay Senter contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Jay Senter contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Educators in Minneapolis have asked the school district for more competitive salaries, better conditions to ensure a more diverse work force and more staff to meet students' mental health needs. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ELIZABETH FLORES/STAR TRIBUNE, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220309ei390003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
