\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Large Majority of Tech Workers at The Times Vote to Certify Their Union\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson \par \uc2 441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ratification, with about 80 percent of ballots in favor, makes it the largest tech union in the United States with collective bargaining rights.\par \par \uc2 Tech workers at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  on Thursday voted in favor of certifying their union in a \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  election, making it one of the biggest tech unions in America.\par \par \uc2 The workers voted in favor, 404 to 88, easily reaching the needed majority of the ballots that were cast. A win means the union, the Times Tech Guild, can begin negotiations for a contract with management.\par \par \uc2 ''We're just elated and really soaking in what this means, not only for us as tech workers at The Times and for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  but also for the tech industry as a whole,'' said Nozlee Samadzadeh, a senior software engineer. ''I think this is going to be the start of a wave of organizing in the tech industry.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Samadzadeh said the union was eager to bargain a contract around issues ''similar to what the newsroom unit has been fighting for -- issues around pay, diversity and equity, a strong contract to make our workplace more fair.''\par \par \uc2 Danielle Rhoades Ha\uc2  , a Times spokeswoman, said The Times looked forward to working with the union to establish a contract.\par \par \uc2 ''We continue to believe this election process was critical so our colleagues could learn more about the union, hear both sides of the argument and, ultimately, make an informed decision,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The Times Tech Guild, which represents about 600 software engineers, product managers, designers, data analysts and other workers, asked The Times for voluntary recognition in April. The Times declined, so the matter went to a formal election through the labor board.\par \par \uc2 The labor board alleged in a complaint in January that The Times's management had violated federal workplace law by preventing some employees from showing support for the union. A Times spokeswoman said at the time that the company disagreed with the allegations.\par \par \uc2 The Times Tech Guild is represented by the NewsGuild of New York, which also represents editorial workers at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and at Wirecutter, the company's product-review website. In 2019, The Times voluntarily recognized the Wirecutter union.\par \par \uc2 The group is the biggest tech union with bargaining rights in America. Hundreds of \uc2 Google\uc2  employees announced a union in January 2021, but it is not registered with the N.L.R.B. and cannot engage in collective bargaining.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Jeenah Moon for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220304ei340004r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
