\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Fact Check\b0\par\par\uc2 948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Factcheck\par \par \uc2 What President Biden said\par \par \uc2 ''In fact, our economy created over 6.5 million new jobs just last year, more jobs created in one year than ever before in the history of America.''\par \par \uc2 This is partly true. Mr. Biden is correct on the numbers, but he is omitting some important context.\par \par \uc2 From his first full month in office in February 2021 to January 2022, the United States economy added 6.6 million jobs, an increase of 4.4 percent. That's indeed the largest job gain as well as fastest growth rate of any president's first full year in office since 1939, when the government began collecting data. (President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  came in second with 3.6 million jobs created in his first year in office, an increase of 4.3 percent.)\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's first year in office, of course, followed historic job losses wrought by the coronavirus pandemic. Employment levels have yet to reach prepandemic highs: About 149.6 million people were employed in January 2022, compared with 152.5 million in February 2020. Most important, presidents are not singularly responsible for the state of the economy. LINDA QIU\par \par \uc2 What President Biden said\par \par \uc2 ''Unlike the $2 trillion tax cut passed in the previous administration that benefited the top 1 percent of Americans, the American Rescue Plan helped working people -- and left no one behind.''\par \par \uc2 This is exagerated. Most Americans, not just those earning the highest incomes, received a tax cut in 2018, despite perceptions to the contrary.\par \par \uc2 The independent \uc2 Tax Policy Center\uc2  estimated that 64.8 percent of people received a federal income tax cut in 2018, while 6.3 saw an increase. About 81.7 percent of Americans who made $50,000 to $75,000 -- roughly a median income -- received a tax cut that averaged $750. That's consistent with estimates from the Joint Committee on Taxation, the nonpartisan analysts of Congress.\par \par \uc2 High-income earners did far better under the tax cut, though, with the top 1 percent receiving nearly 17 percent of the total benefit with an average tax cut of $30,000. LINDA QIU\par \par \uc2 What President Biden said\par \par \uc2 ''Repeal the liability shield that makes gun manufacturers the only industry in America that can't be sued.''\par \par \uc2 This is false. Congress passed a 2005 law prohibiting lawsuits against firearm manufacturers ''for the harm solely caused by the criminal or unlawful misuse of firearm products or ammunition products by others when the product functioned as designed and intended.'' But they can still be subject to other types of lawsuits -- for example, for breaches of warranty or if a manufacturer or dealer sells a gun knowing that it would be used in a crime.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the gun industry is not the only industry to have special protections against lawsuits. For example, technology companies also enjoy a legal shield known as Section 230, which protects websites from liability for content created by its users. LINDA QIU\par \par \uc2 What Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  of Iowa said\par \par \uc2 ''North Korea is testing missiles again at an alarming rate. The speaker of the House recently warned our Olympic athletes not to speak out against China.''\par \par \uc2 This is misleading. Contrary to Ms. Reynolds's suggestion, North Korea continued to sporadically test missiles throughout the Trump administration. In 2018, during diplomatic talks with South Korea and the United States, North Korea said it would suspend missile testing. A timeline compiled by the nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Research Service\uc2  shows that the North refrained from missile tests until May 2019 when it launched a series of short-range ballistic missiles. An analysis from the Center for Strategic and International Studies tallied 30 ballistic missile tests from February 2019, when President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  met in Vietnam with North Korea's leader, Kim Jong-un, to January 2021. North Korea unveiled what appeared to be its largest-ever intercontinental ballistics missile in October 2020.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Reynolds also omitted context in describing the message Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  relayed to American athletes participating in the 2022 Beijing Olympics. Ms. Pelosi did not pressure athletes to remain silent out of deference to China, but rather advised athletes that protesting could be met with reprisal from both the \uc2 International Olympic Committee\uc2  and China's judicial system.\par \par \uc2 ''I know there is a temptation on the part of some to speak out while they are there,'' Ms. Pelosi said in February. ''I respect that. But I also worry about what the Chinese government might do to their reputations, to their families.'' LINDA QIU\par \par \uc2 What Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  of Iowa said\par \par \uc2 ''The Biden administration believes inflation is 'a high-class problem.'''\par \par \uc2 This lacks context. Ms. Reynolds was referring to at an October tweet from \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , President Biden's chief of staff, when he quoted a remark by \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 , a Harvard economist, describing inflation and supply chain issues as ''high-class problems.''\par \par \uc2 Asked about the retweet, the White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , said that critics were missing Mr. Furman's message.\par \par \uc2 ''We're at this point because the unemployment rate has come down and been cut in half, because people are buying more goods, because people are traveling, and because demand is up, and because the economy is turning back on,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Furman echoed that view in an interview on Fox News that month, saying that inflation is real and ''causing a problem for families'' but ''the reason we have this inflation is actually a good reason -- that the unemployment rate has come down, that families got money, and people are buying more things than ever before.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has acknowledged that the impact of inflation, saying last month that inflation ''hurts'' working families. LINDA QIU\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220302ei3200032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
