\par\par \uc2 SpecialSections; SECTMK\par \b \uc2 'Um, My Mom Quit Her Job!'\b0\par\par\uc2 433 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 National\par \par \uc2 'Um, My Mom\par \par \uc2 QUIT her JOB!'\par \par \uc2 was shocked when her mom told her last July that she was quitting her job. Connie, 9, knew that working as a doctor during the pandemic left her mom completely exhausted. Still, she hadn't expected her to quit. ''I was like, wait, is this a dream?'' says Connie, who lives in Akron, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has pushed lots of Americans, including Connie's mom, to leave their jobs, either to move to a different one or to stop working entirely, at least for a while. In November 2021, a record 4.5 million Americans quit or changed their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Klotz, a business professor at Texas A&M University, has come up with a name for the trend: the Great Resignation. (''Resignation'' can mean the act of quitting a job.) He says the pandemic made people think differently about how they want to spend their time, both at work and outside it. For some, the scariness and the losses of the pandemic made them want to spend less time working and more time with their families or doing what they loved. Others quit in-person jobs for positions they could do virtually, instead of going into an office. And some frontline workers, like Connie's mom, just got really tired and needed a change.\par \par \uc2 Many parents also left their jobs to care for their kids after schools went remote. When Jordan Wahle, 6, was starting virtual kindergarten in August 2020, her mom, Jonna, quit her job at a boarding school so she could be there for her. ''I liked that my mom was around me, in case I needed help,'' says Jordan, who lives in Reisterstown, Md.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes the change can take some getting used to. After Connie's mom quit, ''it was actually kind of scary at the beginning,'' Connie says, especially because she was worried about money. But after a while, ''I felt a little bit more calm.'' Her mom plans to go back to work in the future, so Connie is soaking up all the extra time she gets to spend with her now, doing things like playing board games in the afternoon. ''I actually feel happier being with her more,'' she says.\par \par \uc2 Jordan's mom has already returned to work. But Jordan and her mom still get to spend time together during the day -- because Jonna is now a substitute technology teacher at her school. ''I can peek in and give her a big hug,'' Jordan says. Best job perk ever. \u226\'00\'E2\u8212\'20\'14\u352\'01\'60\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220130ei1u0006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
