\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Judge Lifts Order Stopping Wisconsin Hospital Workers From Starting New Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eduardo Medina \par \uc2 1,098 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  had sought to temporarily prevent seven employees from leaving for jobs at Ascension, saying the departures would jeopardize patient care. A judge sided with the workers on Monday.\par \par \uc2 A judge in Wisconsin on Monday lifted an order that had temporarily blocked seven employees of \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2 , a major regional hospital system, from leaving for new jobs with another health care network until it could find people to replace them.\par \par \uc2 The dismissal of a temporary injunction cleared the way for the workers to begin their new jobs with Ascension Northeast Wisconsin. Last week, \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  sued Ascension, seeking to temporarily keep the workers from leaving and touching off an unusual labor dispute rooted in twin crises roiling the health care industry: a shortage of workers, many of whom are demanding higher wages, and a raging coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Ascension Northeast Wisconsin said in a statement before Monday's hearing that \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  ''had an opportunity but declined to make competitive counter offers to retain its former employees.''\par \par \uc2 The employees, members of \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2 's interventional radiology and cardiovascular team, were at-will employees and were not contractually obligated to stay with \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  for a fixed time, according to Ascension, which is part of one of the largest Catholic health care systems in the United States.\par \par \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2 , which operates seven hospitals and provides care to more than 600,000 people annually, said in its lawsuit that it was seeking to ''protect the community'' by temporarily retaining the employees, who accepted new jobs with Ascension in December and were supposed to start on Monday.\par \par \uc2 It added that the employees, who together make up a majority of an 11-person team, provide ''vital care for critically ill patients'' and that Ascension ''should have known that this action would decimate \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2 's ability to provide critical care'' to trauma and stroke victims in the Fox River Valley, a three-county stretch from Green Bay to Oshkosh.\par \par \uc2 Lynn Detterman, a senior vice president of ThedaCare South Region, said in a statement on Monday, ''We know this situation has put the team members who decided to leave \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  in the middle of a difficult situation.''\par \par \uc2 ''Our goal was always to create a short-term orderly transition, not to force team members to continue working at \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2 ,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 David Muth, a lawyer for Ascension, said in a motion filed on Monday that \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  was blaming others for its own mistakes, and that it had attempted to turn its ''poor management'' into ''a disruptive personal emergency for everyone -- anyone -- but itself.''\par \par \uc2 Last week, Judge Mark McGinnis of Outagamie County Circuit Court granted \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2 's request for a temporary restraining order blocking the employees from starting at Ascension this week as planned, and told the lawyers for both parties on Friday to seek a deal, The Post-Crescent of Appleton, Wis., reported.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit was filed as hospital systems across the country, including in Wisconsin, are struggling to retain workers during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But Joe Veenstra, a labor and employment lawyer in La Crosse, Wis., said the lawsuit was an unusual and far-reaching attempt by \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  to interfere with the free market and to keep employees without having to pay them higher wages.\par \par \uc2 ''We've definitely entered an alternate universe,'' Mr. Veenstra said, adding: ''Now we have managements incapable of controlling labor and asking courts to prevent the free market from happening. It's just, we're living in an upside down world right now.''\par \par \uc2 It was unclear how long \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  wanted to retain the seven employees. The hospital system said in its lawsuit that it wanted Ascension to either lend one radiology technician and one nurse to \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  each a day until it hired adequate staff or pause its hiring of the employees until replacements could be found.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Veenstra said that for \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  ''to restrict their employment in this way, it's very, very unusual.''\par \par \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  says in the lawsuit that in order to retain the Level II trauma center status at ThedaCare Regional Medical Center-Neenah -- the second-highest category a hospital can achieve -- it must be able to perform interventional radiology procedures 24 hours a day. That is impossible to sustain if the employees leave, it says.\par \par \uc2 If the hospital is unable to provide round-the-clock interventional radiology care, such as restoring blood flow to a patient's brain after a stroke, it would ''risk the withdrawal of its Level II trauma center verification'' and be forced to transport patients elsewhere, the lawsuit states.\par \par \uc2 Timothy Breister, one of the employees mentioned in the lawsuit, said in a letter to Judge McGinnis that Ascension ''in no way recruited any of the seven of us,'' as \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  has argued.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Mr. Breister said, one member of the team ''received an outstanding offer not just in pay but also a better work/life balance,'' inspiring the others to apply.\par \par \uc2 After they received offers from Ascension, the seven employees asked \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2 's management to match Ascension's offer, Mr. Breister said. He said they were told that ''by matching the offers, the long term expense to \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  was not worth the short term cost and that no counter offer would be made.''\par \par \uc2 He added that patient care would not be compromised because the services the workers performed at \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  would now be done at Ascension. He and the other employees could not be immediately reached on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Detterman of \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  said in an affidavit that if the employees left, the hospital would need to divert patients to facilities as far away as Milwaukee or Madison, both of which are about 100 miles away.\par \par \uc2 ''Unfortunately, it is foreseeable that some patients may die because \uc2 ThedaCare\uc2  cannot timely treat them without interventional radiology and cardiovascular services,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 She added that diverting patients had become more difficult during the pandemic, which has strained hospitals as case counts have risen.\par \par \uc2 Health care workers in Wisconsin, where 63 percent of eligible people are fully vaccinated against Covid-19, are dealing with a surge in cases. On Sunday, the daily average for new cases in the state was more than 21,000, according to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  database.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Muth, the lawyer for Ascension, said in his motion that the company had posted the jobs in part to address the ''same staffing shortages affecting the entire health care industry during the pandemic.''\par \par \uc2 ThedaCare Regional Medical Center-Neenah, a Level II trauma center in Neenah, Wis.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Google Maps FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220125ei1p0005d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
