\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Defying \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , Afghan Women Take to Streets\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Zucchino and Yaqoob Akbary \par \uc2 1,493 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have begun cracking down harder as women insist on their rights and as Western governments call for reforms.\par \par \uc2 KABUL, Afghanistan -- On a raw January morning, Khujasta Elham trudged through a snowstorm to sign her name on a government register.\par \par \uc2 Before the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  seized power in August, Ms. Elham was director of women's programs for Afghanistan's Civil Service Commission. But she and most other female government workers were prevented from returning to work by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's new Islamic Emirate.\par \par \uc2 Now Ms. Elham, who says she has not been paid since August, is required to sign in at her old job site once a month -- a fiction that allows the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to deny that they have fired female government workers. The grim routine also diminishes any hope for Ms. Elham that she will one day return to work.\par \par \uc2 The dismissal of female workers is one of many indignities that have prompted small bands of women like Ms. Elham to take to the streets in protest, risking beatings or arrest. \uc2 Taliban\uc2  gunmen have pointed weapons at the demonstrators, sprayed them with pepper spray and called them ''whores'' and ''puppets of the West,'' \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  said. Bearing placards and raising their fists, the women have resisted persistent attempts to erase them from public life.\par \par \uc2 The protests rarely last for long. \uc2 Taliban\uc2  enforcers have roughed up women, beaten them and sprayed them with chemical irritants, activists say. Ms. Elham and others say they have received threatening phone calls from intelligence officers, warning them to stay silent or face unspecified ''consequences.''\par \par \uc2 ''He asked me if I knew they had prisons for people like me,'' Ms. Elham said of a Taliban intelligence officer who ordered her to end the demonstrations she has helped to organize.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  continue to demand humanitarian aid and diplomatic recognition, the United States and other countries and international bodies have insisted that Afghanistan's new rulers roll back their limits on women's rights. The issue is a main point of discussion this week as \uc2 Taliban\uc2  delegates have begun meeting with international officials in Oslo, Norway.\par \par \uc2 Among the most dramatic consequences of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover has been the swift reversal of gains made by women for two decades following the U.S.-led invasion that toppled the previous \uc2 Taliban\uc2  government in 2001. Women attended schools and universities and served in Parliament and government. They served in the army, the police force and as lawyers and judges.\par \par \uc2 Women once made up at least a quarter of the government work force. But the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have allowed only a handful of female medical workers and educators to retain their government jobs.\par \par \uc2 Most Afghan girls above the sixth grade have not attended school since August. In September, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  converted the Ministry of Women's Affairs building into offices for the religious morality police. Last month, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  banned women from taking long journeys without a male relative and from using public transport without a hijab, a type of head scarf.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have also targeted activists protesting the restrictions. To avoid arrest, Ms. Elham and other demonstrators say they rotate among safe houses and communicate only by encrypted phone apps.\par \par \uc2 Rokhshana Rezai, 27, a prominent activist, said she once dressed as a man to pass through \uc2 Taliban\uc2  checkpoints after receiving threatening calls from \uc2 Taliban\uc2  officials. But she has continued to attend protests. Video from one recent demonstration shows her defiantly pulling away from a Talib who had grabbed her arm and tried to drag her away.\par \par \uc2 ''We are getting more afraid,'' Ms. Rezai said. ''They are not going to respect our rights and dignity.''\par \par \uc2 Taliban\uc2  officials have said that prior approval was required to hold a protest. But when the women have requested permission, Ms. Rezai said, ''They don't allow it, and they never will.''\par \par \uc2 On Jan. 19, three days after women protested the hijab directive, two activists were taken at gunpoint from homes in Kabul, said Zarifa Yaqoobi, 28, a leader of a group called the Afghan Powerful Women's Movement. Ms. Yaqoobi said the women's family members told her the women were taken away at night by armed men.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Yaqoobi said family members identified the activists as Tamana Zaryab Paryani and Parwana Ebrahim Khel. She said three of Ms. Paryani's sisters also disappeared. The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  attempted to speak to the families directly without success.\par \par \uc2 A video posted on social media shows Ms. Paryani screaming for help and shouting that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  were pounding on her door. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have publicly denied any involvement in the detainment of Ms. Paryani and others.\par \par \uc2 Qari Saeed Khosty, a Ministry of Interior spokesman, said that Ms. Paryani's video had been fabricated ''to create a case'' intended to attract international attention.\par \par \uc2 ''They are liars, and I don't want to talk about it,'' said Gen. Mubeen Khan, a police spokesman in Kabul, said of media reports about the disappearances. In a follow-up call, he told The Times: ''Anyone who disrupts the public must be arrested. An order has gone to all security forces to arrest them and bring them to justice.''\par \par \uc2 The crackdown on women's demonstrations in Afghanistan has raised concerns among human rights organizations. \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  said it represented ''an alarming and unlawful escalation of efforts to suppress peaceful protest and free speech in Afghanistan.'' The United Nations Assistance Mission in Afghanistan has called on the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to provide information about the missing women.\par \par \uc2 Women's rights activists and human rights groups have also called on the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to provide information about the disappearance of Alia Azizi, a prison official in the western city of Herat. Ms. Azizi never returned home from work on Oct. 2.\par \par \uc2 Heather Barr\uc2 , associate director of women's rights at \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2 , said ''the extremely muted response'' from the international community coupled with the crackdowns on the Afghan news media had emboldened the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''This is a sign that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  feel these protests now need to be entirely stopped, whatever level of brutality that takes,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The disappearance of the two female activists was raised by an activist from Afghanistan at a conference that opened in Oslo on Sunday among the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , and representatives from the United States and European nations, The Associated Press reported.\par \par \uc2 For the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , the conference offers a platform to present their new Islamic Emirate as less oppressive than the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  government of the 1990s. For the United States and European nations, the meeting provides an opportunity to confront \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leaders face-to-face to demand improved human rights, an inclusive government, women's rights and other reforms.\par \par \uc2 Afghans critical of the conference have protested outside the foreign ministry in Oslo, saying the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  should not be awarded an international platform.\par \par \uc2 On Jan. 23, Monisa Mubariz, co-founder of the Afghan Powerful Women's Movement, and Ms. Yaqoobi held a brief, clandestine news conference in a private home inside a walled compound. They asked the small group of journalists who attended not to livestream the event for fear of alerting the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , Ms. Mubariz told the journalists, ''Women have been deprived of the right to work and to participate and political and economic life. They are consistently repressed, punished illegally, insulted and humiliated.''\par \par \uc2 Such public criticism only heightened the risk of \uc2 Taliban\uc2  reprisals, Ms. Yaqoobi acknowledged. ''That's why we have to operate in secret,'' she said. ''But we will never stop raising our voices.''\par \par \uc2 Days before the news conference, Ms. Mubariz spoke in a coffee shop's family section, set aside for women to keep them separated from men. Before the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover, several coffee shops in Kabul allowed women to sit and socialize with men -- a quiet symbol of progress that has slowly eroded under the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mubariz said her parents and friends have begged her to cease her protests -- or at least to wear body armor to protect herself. She wiped away tears as she described the overwhelming sense of loss she felt since losing her job and watching women's rights being stripped away.\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Rezai, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  threats and crackdowns have eroded her once boundless faith in Afghanistan's future.\par \par \uc2 ''Whatever goals, freedoms, wishes, dreams, choices, education and jobs women once had are gone,'' she said. ''I am feeling angry -- my body is without a soul and all our dreams are nothing now.''\par \par \uc2 At a clandestine news conference in Kabul on Sunday, one woman said they would ''never stop raising our voices.''; KHUJASTA ELHAM, a government worker who said she was threatened by a Taliban intelligence officer. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JIM HUYLEBROEK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); A woman confronted a heckler in October during a protest in a Kabul park in support of women's rights. (PHOTOGRAPH BY VICTOR J. BLUE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220125ei1p0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
