\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Can't Find a Covid Test? Why Some Workers Can.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg, Lauren Hirsch and David McCabe \par \uc2 1,697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the absence of a national effort to make coronavirus testing widely available, a number of big American companies ramped up their own, making tests available for a select group of workers.\par \par \uc2 The latest Covid-19 wave has left millions of Americans scrambling for tests, braving long lines in the cold at pop-up sites or searching furiously online for kits to use at home. But for a select group of employees at some of the country's largest companies, tests are free and often readily available.\par \par \uc2 Without an adequate federal system for developing and distributing rapid tests, companies have put their own testing services in place.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  will send full-time employees in the United States free at-home tests that deliver results within minutes and retail for more than $70 each. \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , an investment firm that manages nearly $10 trillion in assets, offers tele-health supervision as employees self-administer rapid tests for international travel. At \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , bankers, including those at its retail sites, can order at-home rapid tests from an internal company site.\par \par \uc2 Some companies are using the tests to call their staff back to the office. For others, at-home Covid testing has become the newest wellness benefit, a perk to keep employees healthy and working -- even from their couches -- while providing peace of mind.\par \par \uc2 The testing available to a small number of white-collar professionals underscores the difference between their pandemic experience and that of other Americans, putting them at an advantage over many, including workers at small businesses without the means to procure testing kits for their staffs. Like personal protective equipment and vaccines, tests have become the latest example of how a tool to battle the pandemic can exacerbate social and economic divides.\par \par \uc2 ''We're the epicenter of the epicenter and I can't get test kits anywhere,'' said Thomas Grech, president of the Queens Chamber of Commerce, which has roughly 1,400 members that employ about 150,000 workers in the borough.\par \par \uc2 Some employers secured contracts with companies that supply or administer tests in the earlier months of the pandemic, before the Omicron variant unexpectedly drove up demand. Following the advice of experts, some are incorporating testing as part of their return-to-office protocols.\par \par \uc2 Belle Haven Investments, an asset management firm in Westchester County in New York with only 40 employees, has been storing tests in a supply closet.\par \par \uc2 ''We're trying to stockpile them,'' said Laura Chapman, chief operating officer of the firm, which has not mandated a return to the office, though many workers have voluntarily come back. She added that the company was ordering only as many tests as employees were demanding, and that they were facing shortages: ''Those tests, man, those home tests are so hard to get.''\par \par \uc2 In the United States, the federal government has moved more slowly than other countries to authorize rapid antigen tests for everyday use. Britain, for example, was quicker to approve rapid tests as a public health tool, leading to faster production. And unlike Washington's approach to vaccines, the development of rapid tests has until recently been mostly financed by private companies like \uc2 Abbott Laboratories\uc2 . The result is a nationwide shortage of tests.\par \par \uc2 Americans who cannot get tests are often left to wait in lines that can run as long as three hours. Or they can try to buy at-home tests online or in stores. \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS last month announced limits on the purchase of at-home rapid test kits at stores.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has stepped up its efforts to make tests more broadly available and affordable, requiring insurer reimbursement for tests, invoking the Defense Production Act and announcing plans to ship 500 million tests to Americans. The United States is expected to have one billion rapid tests by the end of this year, or three tests per person, according to Tinglong Dai, a professor at Johns Hopkins Carey Business School. The country is now reporting more than two million tests a day on average, up from about 500,000 last summer and higher than at any previous point in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Allen, an associate professor at the \uc2 Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health\uc2 , said earlier coordination on the national level could have ''flooded the market'' with tests and made them more available for everyone.\par \par \uc2 ''It doesn't surprise me that many organizations who were recognizing they need these tests to stay in business were buying them,'' Dr. Allen said.\par \par \uc2 But with testing kits scarce, and sorely needed by people who can't work remotely, some public health experts question the current distribution of resources.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a few better targets than at-home white-collar workers,'' said Dr. Benjamin Mazer, a pathologist in Connecticut specializing in laboratory medicine.\par \par \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , which has more than 7,600 U.S. employees and has extended its work-from-home flexibility through Jan. 28, offers its staff up to one at-home P.C.R. testing kit each week, up to six monthly at-home antigen kits for employees or their family members exposed to Covid, and tele-health supervision for self-administered rapid tests needed for international travel, an option begun over the December holidays.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , bankers can receive up to four free BinaxNOW tests, which cost about $40 in stores, every two weeks through a third party, though the shortage of tests has delayed shipment arrival. At \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , where corporate employees said they were told last month that they could temporarily work from home because of the fast-spreading Omicron variant, bankers can order rapid tests.\par \par \uc2 TIAA, an investment firm with 12,000 workers in the United States, began offering free at-home testing to its staff in December 2020. The majority of its employees have worked from home since the start of the pandemic, though roughly 5 percent had been coming into the office last year. There is no limit on the number of tests employees can order, for themselves as well as their family, according to a spokeswoman, Jessica Scott.\par \par \uc2 ''The goal of offering that is providing comfort to our employees so they don't have to unnecessarily worry or be burdened by having to find a test outside the home,'' said Sean Woodroffe, the firm's head of human resources.\par \par \uc2 TIAA is considering expanding its testing options, by exploring a partnership with the company Detect, which makes at-home molecular tests. ''We're not hoarding tests,'' Mr. Woodroffe added. ''We have tests to fulfill our demand.''\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's full-time employees in the United States have access to multiple types of coronavirus tests they can take at home, the company said. Since last year, employees have been able to request P.C.R. tests provided by a company called \uc2 BioIQ\uc2 . Employees collect a nasal swab at home, and it is processed in the company's lab.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  also distributes to employees who want one a small testing device that produces results in minutes.\par \par \uc2 To perform the rapid molecular test, employees insert a cartridge into the reader, swab their nose and place the swab in the cartridge for results sent by phone. The test is made by \uc2 Cue Health\uc2 , a company that provided testing for the \uc2 National Basketball Association\uc2 's bubble.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  signed a deal with \uc2 Cue Health\uc2  in April to provide its employees with the test kits, according to a securities filing. The devices did not become available to the public for direct purchase until November. \uc2 Cue Health\uc2  also had a prior contract with the federal government and assisted it in distributing the tests across 20 states.\par \par \uc2 Consumers can now buy one of the readers directly from \uc2 Cue Health\uc2  for $250, and a 10-pack of the test cartridges retails for $712. \uc2 Google\uc2  gives employees 10 tests with the \uc2 Cue Health\uc2  reader, and will cover the cost of up to 20 more test cartridges per employee each month. (\uc2 Cue Health\uc2  said it offered memberships for consumers, which include discounts on tests and the reader.)\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  uses many temporary workers, vendors and contractors who do not get access to the \uc2 Cue Health\uc2  tests, according to documents reviewed by The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . A spokeswoman for Google said temporary workers and vendors may use the at-home P.C.R. tests performed by \uc2 BioIQ\uc2  if they were coming into \uc2 Google\uc2 's American offices.\par \par \uc2 Other technology companies have taken more limited approaches to testing. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  offers free rapid antigen home tests to employees on its campus, said a spokesman, Frank Shaw. Meta, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's parent company, provides testing at around 10 of its offices for employees who have returned for in-person work, said Tracy Clayton, a press officer for the company.\par \par \uc2 But for many businesses, and their workers, tests are far harder to come by.\par \par \uc2 Jesus Caicedo-Diaz, who owns Skal, a restaurant in Brooklyn, said his employees were struggling to get Covid test results before the business opened at 10 a.m., with testing lines often running hours long by early morning.\par \par \uc2 Finding at-home tests is an even greater challenge. ''They're nowhere to be found. They're all gone. If you do find them, they want $30 for them,'' Mr. Caicedo-Diaz said. ''If you go to a test site they tell you your result won't come on time. I don't know how to navigate this. It's driving me crazy.''\par \par \uc2 Belle Haven Investments makes at-home Covid tests available to employees. ''We're trying to stockpile them,'' said Laura Chapman, the company's chief operating officer, adding, ''Those tests, man, those home tests are so hard to get.'' (B1); Laura Chapman of Belle Haven Investments said the firm was ordering only as many tests as employees required. PHOTOGRAPHS BY BRYAN DERBALLA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); The giant pharmacy chains Walgreens and CVS have placed limits on the purchase of at-home rapid test kits.(PHOTOGRAPH BY NICOLE CRAINE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Google will give its employees 20 Cue Health cartridges every month. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CUE HEALTH) (B4) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220114ei1d0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
