\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Here to Help; Omicron and the Office: Your Questions, Answered\b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It is a tumultuous time in the world of work. With Covid-19 cases soaring, companies are postponing return-to-office plans or putting in place new safety policies. We looked at five questions workers are struggling with. Emma Goldberg and Lauren Hirsch\par \par \uc2 HOW CAN YOU STAY SAFE IF YOU'RE STARTING TO GO BACK INTO THE OFFICE? It starts with a high-quality mask because the Omicron variant is so infectious. Don't hesitate to be explicit about your safety preferences with colleagues: Let them know, for example, if you want to stay away from others when eating. Companies can do a lot, too, like investing in ventilation and setting up work environments that allow for social distancing.\par \par \uc2 IF YOU HAVE BEEN GOING INTO THE OFFICE, WHAT SHOULD YOU DO IF YOU FEEL SICK? If you start to experience symptoms -- a scratchy throat or congestion -- you should go get a test. Lab tests, known as P.C.R.s, will offer a more accurate assessment of whether you are infected while rapid antigen tests give a good sense of whether you are contagious.\par \par \uc2 IF YOU HAVE COVID, HOW LONG BEFORE YOU CAN GO BACK TO WORK? \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  recommends you isolate for at least five days. After that, if you are asymptomatic or your symptoms are improving, you can mingle with others if you wear a mask for the next five days. Many public health experts say it is best to wait for at least one negative antigen test before leaving isolation.\par \par \uc2 IF YOU'RE STILL STUCK WORKING FROM HOME, HOW CAN YOU GET YOUR BEST WORK DONE? Create an office-like setup at home. A desk or comfortable chair can help you stay focused and productive. We like to add other touches, like candles and snacks. Don't be shy if you are craving guidance on your work -- reach out to your manager and colleagues. And be compassionate with yourself when circumstances are outside your control.\par \par \uc2 HOW CAN YOU CONNECT WITH YOUR COLLEAGUES WHILE WORKING REMOTELY? Don't write off Zoom and Google Hangouts as options to stay connected. Schedule virtual coffee chats with colleagues you want to get to know. Or try an old-fashioned phone call if you're Zoomed out, like us! Send notes to co-workers who have done work that you appreciate. When it's safe, meet up with colleagues outside. We're both big fans of a walk in the park.\par \par \uc2 For more business news, visit nytimes.com/business.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEENAH MOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220112ei1c00050\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
