\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2  Loses Court Fight On Papers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Daisuke Wakabayashi \par \uc2 624 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The latest tranche totals about 200. They are among more than 1,000 that the company, accused of firing union organizers, claimed were protected by attorney-client privilege.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  wrongly claimed attorney-client privilege to protect documents subpoenaed in a \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  case filed by former employees who say the company fired them because of their unionization efforts, a labor judge has ruled.\par \par \uc2 The administrative law judge, Paul Bogas, whom the N.L.R.B. appointed as a special master to review the documents, said in a report on Friday that ''this broad assertion is, to put it charitably, an overreach.''\par \par \uc2 The ruling is the latest legal blow to \uc2 Google\uc2 's defense against a complaint, brought by the labor agency in December 2020, that said the company illegally fired and surveilled employees who were involved in labor organizing.\par \par \uc2 A Google spokeswoman, Jennifer Rodstrom, said in a statement on Monday that the matter had nothing to do with unionization but was about employees breaching security protocols. ''We disagree with the characterization of the legally privileged materials referred to by the complainants,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Judge Bogas ruled in November that \uc2 Google\uc2  had improperly characterized 71 of 80 documents sought by the former employees as privileged. The latest report covers around 200 additional documents pertaining to communications around \uc2 Google\uc2 's hiring of IRI Consultants, a firm known for its anti-union work, as part of Project Vivian, an effort to fight labor organizing at the company.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  must hand over nearly all of those 200 documents, Judge Bogas ruled. He also ordered the company to produce for his review more than 1,000 additional documents that it logged as privileged.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's argument that it had the right to withhold the documents was not ''persuasive,'' Judge Bogas said, because IRI assisted \uc2 Google\uc2  with messaging that did not include legal advice.\par \par \uc2 In one document that the judge said did not pass muster for confidentiality, a Google lawyer explained that the company wanted consulting help for Project Vivian ''to engage employees more positively and convince them that unions suck.'' The lawyer provided a long list of areas where IRI could help, including ''understanding the current sentiment around labor organizing/unionization efforts at \uc2 Google\uc2 .'' The lawyer did not mention assistance with legal help.\par \par \uc2 In another document that \uc2 Google\uc2  claimed was privileged, a different Google lawyer offered public relations advice but not legal counsel. The lawyer proposed that the company find a ''respected voice'' to publish an editorial about what a union would look like in a tech workplace to discourage employees of \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  from forming one. A human resources director said that she supported the idea, but that it needed to be done without \uc2 Google\uc2 's fingerprints. IRI then sent a proposed editorial to the Google lawyer.\par \par \uc2 Judge Bogas also chastised \uc2 Google\uc2  for marking documents as privileged just because it copied in a company lawyer, even though the communication did not seek legal advice.\par \par \uc2 ''A company cannot cloak a document in privilege merely by providing a copy to counsel,'' the judge wrote.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reported earlier that \uc2 Google\uc2  encouraged employees to aggressively mark internal communication as ''A/C Priv,'' which is shorthand for ''attorney-client privilege,'' in the subject line even if they are not seeking legal advice.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  denied that was the case, and said it informed employees that they should do that only when appropriate.\par \par \uc2 The ruling is the latest legal blow to Google's defense against a complaint that said the company illegally fired and surveilled employees who were involved in labor organizing. (PHOTOGRAPH BY John Taggart for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220111ei1b00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
