\par\par \uc2 THE HUNT\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 First-Time Buyers Weigh Their Apartment Options in Brooklyn and New Jersey\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joyce Cohen \par \uc2 849 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Not long after the pandemic arrived in New York, Brandon Tarbert faced the decision of whether to leave the city for good.\par \par \uc2 One option was San Francisco, where his company was based. He researched ''sea-level rise'' information on the noaa.gov website to learn how climate change might affect the area. But the Bay Area also carried too much risk of wildfires and earthquakes.\par \par \uc2 ''That sounds like doom-and-gloom planning, but homeowners have to think about these things,'' said Mr. Tarbert, 39, who works in renewable energy.\par \par \uc2 And he wasn't especially enthusiastic about leaving New York. ''There are few other places that have such an eclectic mix of food, art and nightlife,'' he said. Besides, his girlfriend, Angie Tennant, 32, an actor who specializes in androgynous roles, was tethered to the area.\par \par \uc2 Mx. Tennant had recently moved into Mr. Tarbert's home on Flatbush Avenue, in Ditmas Park, Brooklyn, from her apartment in Washington Heights, almost an hour and a half away by public transit. ''It felt like a long-distance relationship,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The Flatbush apartment, a three-bedroom atop a nail salon and a hair-braiding place, was plagued by nonstop traffic noise that made it tough to sleep. The rent was $2,600 for 1,200 square feet, with a terrace. But Mr. Tarbert, who works remotely, was eager to buy a home.\par \par \uc2 With a budget of up to $600,000, the couple began the hunt last year, focusing on neighborhoods they liked in Brooklyn -- and in northern New Jersey, which intrigued Mr. Tarbert when it appeared in his online searches, because costs and taxes were so much lower. Also, a friend from his Peace Corps days lived there.\par \par \uc2 They needed sufficient work-from-home space. ''Brandon has what we have termed the megatron, with three different monitors, a laptop and an electric desk that raises and lowers,'' Mx. Tennant said.\par \par \uc2 They didn't want to relinquish outdoor space, and central air-conditioning was a must. Among their options:\par \par \uc2 1. Co-op in Kensington\par \par \uc2 This 900-square-foot Brooklyn one-bedroom was on a middle floor in a 16-story 1965 building. The apartment, which could be converted to two bedrooms, had a dining or office alcove, an open kitchen and a balcony. The asking price was $549,000, with monthly maintenance of around $1,000.\par \par \uc2 2. Co-op in Union City, N.J.\par \par \uc2 This two-bedroom, two-bathroom was in a 23-story full-service building from 1969, with an outdoor pool and parking deck. It was around 1,100 square feet, with a balcony and views of Manhattan. The price was $475,000, with maintenance of around $1,500 a month (including most utilities).\par \par \uc2 3. Condo in Crown Heights\par \par \uc2 This one-bedroom was in a six-story 2010 building with a tax abatement until 2027. It was around 625 square feet and had a sleeping area sectioned off with frosted glass, as well as an open kitchen, a private keyed elevator, a basement storage unit and a large balcony. The price was $541,000, with monthly charges of around $500.\par \par \uc2 Their Choice\par \par \uc2 The couple liked the Kensington building, where a friend lived, but Mr. Tarbert's low bid of $530,000 was rejected. ''It was a learning moment for me,'' he said. The Crown Heights condo appealed for its price and location. But inside, it felt like a concrete box.\par \par \uc2 So they opted for New Jersey, where their money went much further. ''I am acutely aware of the tax burden of living in New York City,'' Mr. Tarbert said.\par \par \uc2 Via StreetEasy, he contacted Deborah Toney of Keller Williams City Life Hoboken, who showed the couple the Doric, a 440-unit building perched atop the Palisades, overlooking the Hudson River and the Manhattan skyline. ''The building was lovely,'' Mx. Tennant said. ''It felt so open and quiet.''\par \par \uc2 A two-bedroom, in move-in shape with a spectacular view to the east and south, felt right. ''In Brooklyn, this would be a $1.5 million to $2 million apartment,'' said Mr. Tarbert, who paid the asking price. The couple and their dog, Bunji, arrived in the summer.\par \par \uc2 ''I do miss my favorite Chinese delivery place in Brooklyn,'' Mr. Tarbert said, adding, ''But I am a three-minute drive to Trader Joe's and a 10-minute drive to \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2 .'' (His parking spot is $150 a month.)\par \par \uc2 Mx. Tennant, who contributes $820 a month, needed time to warm to New Jersey. ''It was a bit of an identity crisis,'' she said. But practicality, and amenities, won out.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel like, turning 30, I am not cool anymore,'' she said. ''Then, once I got here, I realized there were many things that being out of the city afforded me that were more important than my hipster street cred.''\par \par \uc2 Email thehunt@nytimes.com\par \par \uc2 The Buyers: Brandon Tarbert and Angie Tennant, with their dog, Bunji. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TOM SIBLEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); The Options \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211226ehcq0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
