\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Appeals Court Reinstates OSHA's Vaccine Mandate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Hirsch, Emma Goldberg and Charlie Savage \par \uc2 1,298 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The decision, by a split three-judge panel, overturned a ruling that had blocked a Biden administration rule requiring companies with 100 or more employees to mandate vaccinations or testing for workers.\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals panel on Friday reinstated a Biden administration rule requiring larger companies to mandate that their workers get vaccinated against the coronavirus or submit to weekly testing by early January.\par \par \uc2 The decision, by a split three-judge panel of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit in Cincinnati, overturned a ruling last month by its counterpart in New Orleans, the Fifth Circuit, that had blocked the government from carrying out the rule.\par \par \uc2 The contested rule, issued by the Department of Labor's \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2 , or OSHA, has faced a wave of lawsuits from businesses and Republican-controlled states. Several challengers immediately said they planned to file or already had filed emergency motions with the Supreme Court to block the rule.\par \par \uc2 ''The record establishes that Covid-19 has continued to spread, mutate, kill and block the safe return of American workers to their jobs,'' wrote Judge Jane B. Stranch. ''To protect workers, OSHA can and must be able to respond to dangers as they evolve.''\par \par \uc2 The White House welcomed the decision.\par \par \uc2 ''The OSHA vaccination or testing rule will ensure businesses enact measures that will protect their employees,'' Kevin Munoz, a White House spokesman, said in a statement. ''Especially as the U.S. faces the highly transmissible Omicron variant, it's critical we move forward with vaccination requirements and protections for workers with the urgency needed in this moment.''\par \par \uc2 The National Retail Federation\uc2 , one of the trade groups suing against the mandate, said it would ''consider additional legal options. We will also continue to prepare our members to comply with this onerous mandate.''\par \par \uc2 President Biden announced in September that his administration would develop the rule as one of several steps to try to increase immunization rates and curtail the pandemic, which has killed more than 800,000 Americans so far. Other mandates applied to federal employees and federal contractors.\par \par \uc2 The rule for large employers, which OSHA issued last month, forces such companies to require vaccinations by Jan. 4 and to require unvaccinated employees to wear masks indoors.\par \par \uc2 Employers also are allowed to give their workers the option to be tested weekly instead of getting the vaccine, though they are not required to do so unless the worker has an exemption for medical or religious reasons. The rule makes an exception for employees who do not come into close contact with other people at their jobs, such as those who work at home or exclusively outdoors.\par \par \uc2 The administration estimated that 22 million people would get vaccinated and that 250,000 hospitalizations would be prevented because of the rule, which applies to more than 84 million workers.\par \par \uc2 Under a 1970 law, OSHA has the authority to pass a so-called emergency temporary standard, imposing new rules for workplace safety, provided it can show that workers are exposed to a ''grave danger'' and that the rule is necessary.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit panel ruled last month that the agency had exceeded that authority, blocking the government from carrying out the mandate. But the case was then reassigned to the Sixth Circuit, which was randomly chosen to consolidate numerous challenges against the rule that had been filed around the country.\par \par \uc2 In dissolving the Fifth Circuit's block on Friday, the majority of the Sixth Circuit panel said that the agency had sufficiently demonstrated that measures against the coronavirus pandemic meet the standard laid out by Congress in the 1970 law that created the agency.\par \par \uc2 ''OSHA has demonstrated the pervasive danger that Covid-19 poses to workers -- unvaccinated workers in particular -- in their workplaces,'' Judge Stranch wrote, adding that the possibility of new variants cited by the agency when it issued the rule ''has borne out with the Omicron variant.''\par \par \uc2 The majority opinion also rejected a constitutional challenge to the rule.\par \par \uc2 Judge Stranch, an Obama appointee, was joined by Judge Julia Smith Gibbons, who was appointed by President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . In a brief concurring opinion, Judge Gibbons signaled that as a matter of judicial restraint the courts should defer to OSHA's expertise.\par \par \uc2 ''Reasonable minds may disagree on OSHA's approach to the pandemic, but we do not substitute our judgment for that of OSHA, which has been tasked by Congress with policymaking responsibilities,'' she wrote, adding: ''Beyond constitutional limitations, the work of an agency, often scientific and technical in nature, is outside our expertise.''\par \par \uc2 But Judge Joan L. Larsen, a Trump appointee, dissented, arguing -- as had the Fifth Circuit panel before her -- that the agency had exceeded its legal authority.\par \par \uc2 ''The mandate is aimed directly at protecting the unvaccinated from their own choices,'' Judge Larsen wrote. ''Vaccines are freely available, and unvaccinated people may choose to protect themselves at any time. And because the secretary likely lacks congressional authority to force them to protect themselves, the remaining stay factors cannot tip the balance.''\par \par \uc2 All of the judges on the Fifth Circuit panel that had blocked the rule were conservative Republican appointees.\par \par \uc2 Challengers to the decision could appeal directly to the Supreme Court, which is controlled by a conservative bloc of six Republican appointees. (The Supreme Court this month refused to block New York's requirement that health care workers be vaccinated against the coronavirus even when they cite religious objections.)\par \par \uc2 Challengers could also appeal to the full U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit. Of its 16 sitting judges, five were appointed by Democrats and 11 were appointed by Republicans. (However, one of the Republican appointees, Judge Helene N. White, was originally a nominee of a Democratic president, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , before being renominated by a Republican one, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , as part of a political deal.)\par \par \uc2 Conditions on the ground are rapidly changing, with new cases surging, apparently because of the more-infectious Omicron variant. The Justice Department last month warned that keeping the mandate from coming into effect ''would likely cost dozens or even hundreds of lives per day, in addition to large numbers of hospitalizations, other serious health effects and tremendous costs.''\par \par \uc2 The OSHA rule, alongside a separate requirement for federal contractors, has helped drive a number of large companies to announce a form of vaccine mandate, including \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2 , \uc2 IBM\uc2  and \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 . Others, like \uc2 Tyson Foods\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 , introduced mandates on their own, in the face of the rising risk of the Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 But while some companies welcomed the OSHA requirement as a cover to move forward, others have resisted, citing concerns about exacerbating an already-challenging labor shortage. Some of the largest private employers, including \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , have yet to outline a broad mandate for all employees.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration's rule requiring vaccinations for federal contractors and their employees remains held up in litigation, and a number of employers, including \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 , \uc2 General Electric\uc2  and \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 , have suspended their vaccine requirements.\par \par \uc2 A recent poll of 543 companies by the consulting firm Willis \uc2 Towers Watson\uc2  found that 57 percent of firms either required or planned to require Covid-19 vaccinations. That included 32 percent that planned to mandate vaccines only if the OSHA rule takes effect. Seven percent said they planned to carry it out regardless of the outcome.\par \par \uc2 The mandate, issued by OSHA, has faced a wave of lawsuits from businesses, trade groups and states. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JUSTIN SULLIVAN/GETTY IMAGES); The rule orders that businesses with at least 100 workers mandate Covid vaccinations or give workers the option to be tested for the virus weekly. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MATT ROURKE/ASSOCIATED PRESS) (B4) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211218ehci00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
