\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  Shareholders Vote to Go Public, as Union Workers in News Division Stage Walkout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson and Peter Eavis \par \uc2 690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  will make its market debut as soon as Monday. As shareholders voted on the deal, some of the media company's employees staged a work stoppage.\par \par \uc2 Shareholders in \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2 , the digital media pioneer known for its listicles, quizzes and a news division that won its first Pulitzer Prize this year, voted on Thursday to take the company public.\par \par \uc2 The deal that will take \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  onto the stock market raised less money than initially expected, which could crimp the company's spending in the years to come and lead it to rein in its ambitions.\par \par \uc2 The long-in-the-works plan, led by the BuzzFeed co-founder and chief executive \uc2 Jonah Peretti\uc2 , will merge it with a special purpose acquisition company, 890 5th Avenue Partners. So-called SPACs raise money through an initial public offering and use that cash to buy a private company.\par \par \uc2 The deal is expected to close by Friday, 890 5th Avenue Partners said in a news release. \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  will make its stock market debut as soon as Monday, under the ticker symbol BZFD. Part of the deal includes the completion of \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2 's $300 million acquisition of Complex Networks, a sports and entertainment publisher.\par \par \uc2 Because investors who buy into a SPAC do not know what company it plans to buy, they have the opportunity to redeem those shares at their I.P.O. price -- in this case $10 -- before it reaches any deal. Many did. \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  could have raised over $250 million from the investors in the SPAC, but in the end it got only $16 million, according to a news release from \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  and 890. But \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  will have $150 million that it is raising from selling a debt security. Other SPAC deals in recent weeks have suffered from shareholders asking for their money back.\par \par \uc2 As the shareholders were casting their votes, a move that could mean millions of dollars for its early investors and some current and former staff members, not everyone at the company was cheering: Union employees at its news division, \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  News, staged a daylong work stoppage in an effort to speed contract negotiations. All 61 of the workers who belong to the BuzzFeed News Union, which includes reporters, editors and designers, took part, the union said.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the union accused the company of refusing to budge in contract negotiations. The main sticking point is pay. The union said \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  was proposing a 1 percent guaranteed annual wage increase and a minimum salary of $50,000.\par \par \uc2 ''We deserve a strong contract that protects us and ensures a fair and equitable workplace for everyone in our unit,'' Katie Notopoulos, a senior tech reporter, said in the statement.\par \par \uc2 A BuzzFeed spokesman said the company would be back at the negotiating table ''next Tuesday where we hope the union will present a response on these issues.''\par \par \uc2 The union, which formed in February 2019, is represented by the NewsGuild, which also represents workers at The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and other media outlets. The union and the company have yet to agree on a first contract.\par \par \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  was started out of a small office in New York's Chinatown in 2006 as an experimental project in viral media for Mr. Peretti, back when his day job was chief technology officer of The Huffington Post. He devoted himself full time to \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  in 2011, after \uc2 AOL\uc2  bought HuffPost for $315 million, and transformed it into a stand-alone media company with the help of $35 million from investors.\par \par \uc2 It was soon hailed as the future of the news media. In recent years, though, it has missed revenue targets, and some investors pushed for a sale. Last year, \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  gained scale when it acquired HuffPost from its last owner, \uc2 Verizon\uc2 , in a stock deal.\par \par \uc2 BuzzFeed's headquarters in New York in 2018. All 61 employees who belong to the BuzzFeed News Union stopped work on Thursday as shareholders voted on the listing. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Drew Angerer/Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211203ehc300034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
